(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["stations"] = {"case-comms-cancelled-child":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Amira, aged 5</strong>, is listed for tonsillectomy this morning for recurrent tonsillitis. On your preoperative visit she has a temperature of 38.2°C, a moist cough and coarse crackles on the right. You have decided with your consultant to <strong>postpone</strong> her surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> explain this decision to her father, <strong>Mr Hassan</strong> (a role player), and answer his questions.</p>","scenario":"<p>Mr Hassan has taken two days off work, the family has waited 9 months, and Amira has fasted since last night. He is frustrated and asks why she cannot \"just have the operation since she's here\". He worries she will keep getting tonsillitis. If the candidate is empathetic and clear he becomes calmer and asks when she can have the operation.</p>","prompts":"<p>(Role player cues)</p>\n<ul><li>\"We've waited nine months for this — can't you just do it?\"</li><li>\"It's only a cough; she always has a cough.\"</li><li>\"What exactly is the risk?\"</li><li>\"So when can she have it?\"</li></ul>","features":["Introduces themself and checks the parent's understanding and concerns","Acknowledges his frustration and apologises for the disruption","Explains the reason clearly without jargon (chest infection with fever and chest signs)","Explains the specific risks (breathing problems during and after anaesthesia such as airway spasm, low oxygen levels, pneumonia)","Balances the decision: why waiting is safer now","Gives a plan: treatment, return timing (typically after a few weeks once well), rebooking priority, who will contact them","Addresses practical issues (eating now, work letter if needed)","Checks understanding and offers further questions; remains calm and respectful"],"domains":"<p>Empathy, compassion and respect; explanation and articulation; honesty and integrity (not minimising the risk or the inconvenience).</p>","model":"<p>\"I can see how frustrating this is after such a long wait, and I'm sorry.\" A simple explanation of airway irritability and infection, a clear, honest statement of risk, and a concrete plan (antibiotics if prescribed by the GP/team, reassessment, rebooking with priority), ending with a check that Mr Hassan feels his concerns have been heard.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Postpone for systemic illness, fever, productive cough or chest signs.</li><li>Airway hyper-reactivity can persist for several weeks after an URTI.</li><li>Communication stations reward structure and empathy as much as facts.</li></ul>"},"case-critical-sepsis-referral":{"candidate":"<p>You are the anaesthetic resident on call. You are asked to see <strong>Mr Evans, 67</strong>, on the surgical ward, 3 days after an anterior resection. His NEWS2 score is 9.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> interpret the information provided, say what immediate treatment you would start, and then telephone the ICU consultant (the examiner) with a structured referral.</p>","scenario":"<p>Observations: RR 28, SpO<sub>2</sub> 91% on 4 L/min nasal cannulae, HR 124 (sinus), BP 84/46 mmHg, temperature 38.9°C, new confusion. Abdomen distended and tender. Urine output 60 mL in 6 hours.</p>\n<p>Arterial blood gas on 4 L/min: pH 7.26, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.9 kPa, PaO<sub>2</sub> 8.1 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 13 mmol/L, BE −12, lactate 6.2 mmol/L, glucose 9.8.</p>\n<p>Bloods: WCC 21, CRP 310, creatinine 168 (baseline 80), platelets 95.</p>\n<p>Past history: COPD (mild), hypertension. He has said to his family he \"would want everything done\".</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(2 min) \"Talk me through the blood gas.\"</li><li>(4 min) \"What are you going to do in the next hour?\"</li><li>(5 min) Play the ICU consultant: \"Hello, ICU consultant here — what have you got?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"Does he need surgery? What happens next?\"</li></ul>","features":["Recognises septic shock (hypotension, lactate &gt; 2) with likely anastomotic leak","Interprets the gas: metabolic (lactic) acidosis with respiratory compensation and type 1 respiratory failure","Starts oxygen titrated to target, IV access, cultures, broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour, fluid resuscitation with reassessment","Plans vasopressor support and arterial monitoring (critical care)","Identifies the need for source control (surgical review, CT) and informs the surgical team","Gives a structured SBAR referral with the key numbers and the patient's wishes","Considers ceiling of care and communicates with the family"],"domains":"<p>Information gathering, clinical decision-making and prioritisation, information sharing (structured referral).</p>","model":"<p>A concise summary: \"septic shock from a probable anastomotic leak with AKI and hypoxaemic respiratory failure; NEWS 9, lactate 6.2, MAP 59 despite 1 L\". Actions already started (antibiotics, fluids, oxygen), a request for ICU admission for vasopressors and monitoring, and an urgent surgical decision about CT and laparotomy. States the patient's expressed wishes.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Sepsis-3: septic shock = vasopressor need to keep MAP ≥ 65 with lactate &gt; 2 despite fluids.</li><li>Antibiotics and source control are time-critical.</li><li>SBAR makes referrals clear and fast.</li></ul>"},"case-equipment-machine-check":{"candidate":"<p>You are about to start a morning list. Your consultant (the examiner) asks you to talk through the <strong>pre-use check of the anaesthetic machine</strong> in front of you, and to explain any problems you find.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> perform and explain the check; identify and explain the significance of any faults.</p>","scenario":"<p>The examiner uses a real or simulated machine with <strong>three planted faults</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li>The <strong>oxygen pipeline hose is not fully engaged</strong> (fails the tug test; pipeline gauge reads 0 while the cylinder is on).</li><li>The <strong>circle system has a leak</strong>: the reservoir bag does not stay inflated on the leak test with the APL closed (an absorber canister not seated).</li><li>The <strong>soda lime has turned violet</strong> (exhausted).</li></ol>\n<p>The oxygen analyser, suction and monitors are working.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(Start) \"Take me through your check.\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Why do we do a tug test? What would happen if we didn't?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"How does the oxygen failure alarm work?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"Explain the chemistry of CO<sub>2</sub> absorption. Why does the colour change?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What would you do if you had to start an emergency case now with a faulty machine?\"</li></ul>","features":["Uses a structured approach consistent with the Association of Anaesthetists' checking guidance","Checks gas supplies: pipelines with tug test, cylinder contents, pressure gauges","Identifies the disengaged oxygen hose and its risk (running on cylinder, silent depletion)","Checks the oxygen analyser, flowmeters and hypoxic guard, and the vaporisers (seated, filled, locked)","Performs a breathing system leak and function (two-bag) test and identifies the leak","Identifies exhausted absorbent and explains rebreathing and rising inspired CO<sub>2</sub>","Explains the oxygen failure alarm (powered by oxygen pressure, audible, cuts off other gases)","Confirms alternative means of ventilation (self-inflating bag and oxygen cylinder), suction and monitoring","Knows not to use a faulty machine and how to escalate (another machine, TIVA with a self-inflating bag and portable oxygen)"],"domains":"<p>Technical skills (systematic check), clinical reasoning (linking faults to physics and patient risk), assessment and reduction of risk.</p>","model":"<p>A calm, logical check from supply to patient, verbalising why each step matters, finding all three faults, and refusing to proceed until they are fixed; explaining that CO<sub>2</sub> reacts with hydroxides to form carbonates, water and heat, with a pH-sensitive indicator changing colour.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Most machine-related incidents are detectable by a proper check.</li><li>A two-bag test confirms the whole breathing system works with a \"test lung\".</li><li>The ultimate backup is a self-inflating bag and an oxygen cylinder.</li></ul>"},"case-intraop-hypoxia":{"candidate":"<p>You are the anaesthetist for a <strong>45-year-old man (ASA 2, smoker)</strong> having an elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy. He has been induced with propofol, fentanyl and rocuronium and intubated by your colleague, who has now left theatre. The ODP is with you.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> manage the situation that develops. Talk through what you are thinking and doing.</p>","scenario":"<p>On the manikin (SimMan) or with a simulated monitor, SpO<sub>2</sub> falls from 99% to 88% over 2 minutes after the surgeon starts insufflation and the table is tilted head-down. Airway pressures rise from 20 to 34 cmH<sub>2</sub>O; ETCO<sub>2</sub> trace is present but the plateau slopes upwards. Breath sounds (if auscultated) are reduced on the left. If the candidate checks the tube length, it is at <strong>26 cm at the lips</strong> (was 22 cm). Pulling back to 22 cm restores bilateral air entry and SpO<sub>2</sub> recovers to 97%.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) The ODP says: \"The sats are dropping — what do you want me to do?\"</li><li>(3 min) If the candidate fixates on bronchospasm: \"The wheeze is only on the right side…\"</li><li>(6 min) After correction: \"What caused this, and how will you prevent it happening again?\"</li><li>(8 min) Extension: \"If the tube position had been fine, what else would you consider?\"</li></ul>","features":["Calls for help and informs the surgeon early (pause insufflation/tilt)","Increases FiO<sub>2</sub> to 1.0 and hand-ventilates to assess compliance","Uses a systematic check from machine to patient (oxygen supply, circuit, tube, breathing, circulation)","Checks tube position/length and auscultates both sides","Recognises endobronchial intubation caused by head-down tilt and pneumoperitoneum","Withdraws the tube to the correct length and confirms bilateral ventilation and capnography","Considers alternatives: bronchospasm, pneumothorax, aspiration, secretions, anaphylaxis, atelectasis","Uses the QRH or a cognitive aid appropriately and communicates clearly with the team"],"domains":"<p>Response to an evolving emergency, situational awareness, clinical reasoning (applying physiology of shunt and V/Q), team working and communication.</p>","model":"<p>Declares \"we have a desaturation\", asks the surgeon to release the pneumoperitoneum and level the table, switches to 100% O<sub>2</sub> and hand-ventilates, then works methodically, finds the tube has migrated (cephalad movement of the carina with pneumoperitoneum and Trendelenburg), corrects it, and re-secures and documents. Explains the physiology: one-lung ventilation creates a large shunt; oxygen partially improves saturation.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>The carina moves up (and the tube relatively down) with pneumoperitoneum and head-down tilt: check tube length after positioning.</li><li>Always \"COVER ABCD\"-type check: machine to patient.</li><li>Shunt responds poorly to FiO<sub>2</sub>; correct the cause.</li></ul>"},"case-obstetric-spinal":{"candidate":"<p>You are the obstetric anaesthetist. <strong>Mrs Kaur, 32</strong>, term, needs a <strong>category 2 caesarean section</strong> for failure to progress. She does not have an epidural. Your consultant (the examiner) is present and asks you to talk through your anaesthetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> discuss your plan, the anatomy and the management of problems.</p>","scenario":"<p>She is healthy, platelets 210, BMI 29, no allergies. Fetal heart rate is reassuring. She is anxious but keen for a spinal.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"Talk me through your plan.\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Which space will you choose and why? What structures will your needle pass through?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"How will you test the block, and what level do you need?\"</li><li>(6 min) \"Her blood pressure falls to 80/40 after the spinal. What do you do?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"At skin incision she says she can feel sharp pain. What now?\"</li></ul>","features":["Plans WHO checklist, antacid prophylaxis, IV access, consent and team communication","Chooses an interspace at or below L3–4 and explains the conus level and Tuffier's line inaccuracy","Lists layers from skin to subarachnoid space","Describes drugs: hyperbaric bupivacaine with intrathecal opioid(s)","Tests the block bilaterally to touch at T4 (and cold higher) before surgery","Manages hypotension: left uterine displacement, phenylephrine (infusion/boluses), fluids","Manages inadequate block: stop surgery, assess, supplement (e.g. Entonox, IV opioid) only if appropriate, and convert to GA if needed with proper explanation","Communicates with the patient throughout"],"domains":"<p>Planning, explanation and articulation, technical skills (applied anatomy and block assessment).</p>","model":"<p>A safe, confident plan with correct anatomy, a T4 touch block target, prophylactic phenylephrine infusion, uterine displacement, and a clear, patient-centred approach to pain at incision (never ask the woman to \"put up with it\"; offer GA if the block is inadequate).</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Test the block to touch (T4) — a cold level alone may overestimate it.</li><li>Failure to recognise an inadequate block is a common cause of complaints and litigation.</li><li>Phenylephrine is the vasopressor of choice for spinal hypotension at caesarean section.</li></ul>"},"case-outside-mri-child":{"candidate":"<p>Your consultant has asked you to plan the anaesthetic for <strong>Leo, aged 6 (22 kg)</strong>, who needs a 45-minute MRI of the brain for headaches. He has autism and is very anxious; a previous attempt with oral sedation failed.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> discuss your plan with your consultant (the examiner) and answer their questions.</p>","scenario":"<p>Leo is otherwise well, with no airway concerns. His mother will come to the induction room. The MRI unit is on the ground floor, away from theatres, with an MR-conditional anaesthetic machine and monitoring. A 1.5 tesla scanner is used.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"How would you approach this?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Why is the MRI environment hazardous? Explain the physics.\"</li><li>(5 min) \"What monitoring and equipment will you use, and what are the problems with them?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"How would you manage a cardiac arrest in the scanner?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What reasonable adjustments will you make for his autism?\"</li></ul>","features":["Chooses GA (or deep sedation with appropriate airway plan) given failed sedation and scan length","Plans induction outside zone 4 (induction room) with a parent present","Explains the static magnetic field (projectile risk), gradient fields (noise, nerve stimulation) and radiofrequency heating (burns from looped cables)","Screens patient and staff for ferromagnetic objects and implants","Uses MR-conditional machine, monitors and pumps (or long lines through a waveguide) and hearing protection","Recognises monitoring limitations (ECG artefact, capnography delay)","States that resuscitation is done outside the scanner room after removing the patient","Describes reasonable adjustments for autism (quiet room, familiar items, hospital passport, flexible induction, premedication)","Plans recovery and discharge criteria"],"domains":"<p>Planning, assessment of risk, explanation and articulation (explaining physics and safety in plain terms to a colleague).</p>","model":"<p>A clear, safety-focused plan with the physics explained accurately: \"The main field is always on — a steel cylinder becomes a projectile; RF energy heats tissue and loops of cable, so I'll avoid loops and pad contact points.\" Recognises that in an emergency the child is taken out of the scanner room (magnet zone) for resuscitation and defibrillation.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>MR safe / MR conditional / MR unsafe labelling.</li><li>Quench releases helium: asphyxia risk; the magnet is not simply \"switched off\".</li><li>Remote-site anaesthesia requires the same standards as theatre.</li></ul>"},"case-pharm-last":{"candidate":"<p>You are performing an <strong>ultrasound-guided axillary brachial plexus block</strong> on <strong>Mr Chen, 55 (60 kg)</strong>, for a wrist fracture fixation. You have injected <strong>25 mL of 0.5% levobupivacaine</strong> when he says his mouth feels numb and his ears are ringing. An ODP is with you.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> manage the situation and answer the examiner's questions.</p>","scenario":"<p>Within a minute the patient becomes agitated, then has a generalised seizure. On the monitor: HR 45, broad QRS complexes, BP 70/40 mmHg, SpO<sub>2</sub> 90%. If treated appropriately (airway, oxygen, seizure control, lipid), his rhythm and BP improve over several minutes. If the candidate gives large doses of adrenaline, the examiner notes arrhythmias worsen.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) ODP: \"What do you need?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"How much lipid emulsion will you give?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"Why was this patient at risk? Was the dose too high?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"How does lipid work?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What happens after he recovers?\"</li></ul>","features":["Stops injecting and calls for help, gets the lipid emulsion and QRH","Manages the airway with 100% O<sub>2</sub> and ventilation (avoids hypoxia and acidosis)","Controls seizures with small doses of benzodiazepine, propofol or thiopental","Gives 20% lipid emulsion: 1.5 mL/kg bolus (≈ 90 mL), then 15 mL/kg/h infusion, with repeat boluses if needed (maximum about 12 mL/kg)","Supports the circulation (fluids; reduced-dose adrenaline if arrest) and prepares for prolonged CPR","Calculates the dose given (125 mg = about 2.1 mg/kg — at the maximum) and discusses risk factors","Explains mechanisms (sodium channel blockade; lipid sink and metabolic effects)","Plans monitoring, reporting and follow-up (including checking for pancreatitis/lipid effects, and informing the patient)"],"domains":"<p>Response to an emergency, prescribing (accurate lipid dosing), team working.</p>","model":"<p>Immediate recognition of LAST, clear instructions to the ODP, safe airway management, the correct lipid regimen without hesitation, and an explanation that bupivacaine's cardiotoxicity reflects slow dissociation from sodium channels; the S-enantiomer is less toxic but not safe in overdose or intravascular injection.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>The QRH LAST card and lipid should be immediately available wherever blocks are done.</li><li>Maximum doses are guides: vascularity and site matter.</li><li>Propofol is not a substitute for lipid.</li></ul>"},"case-postop-recovery-hypotension":{"candidate":"<p>You are called to recovery to see <strong>Mrs Patel, 84</strong>, who has had a <strong>cemented hemiarthroplasty</strong> for a fractured neck of femur under spinal anaesthesia (hyperbaric bupivacaine 2.2 mL, no intrathecal opioid) with light propofol sedation. She arrived in recovery 20 minutes ago.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> assess her using the information provided by the recovery nurse (the examiner), explain your reasoning, and decide on management.</p>","scenario":"<p>Observations: BP 78/40 mmHg (preop 150/85), HR 58 in sinus rhythm, SpO<sub>2</sub> 94% on 4 L/min oxygen, RR 16, temperature 35.4°C, drowsy but rousable. Sensory block to cold at <strong>T8</strong>. Estimated blood loss 500 mL; she received 1 L of Hartmann's. Hb in theatre was 102 g/L (preop 118). Drug chart: amlodipine, ramipril (taken this morning), bisoprolol. Wound dressing dry; drain 150 mL. Urine output 20 mL in the last hour. The surgeon mentions \"some wobbles\" at cementation.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(2 min) \"What do you think is going on?\"</li><li>(4 min) \"What are you going to do right now?\" (the nurse will do what is asked)</li><li>(6 min) If fluids and vasopressor are given: BP rises to 105/55 mmHg. \"What else would you check?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"She's ready for the ward — are you happy with that?\"</li></ul>","features":["Assesses ABC promptly and confirms the reading (cuff size, manual check)","Gives a fluid bolus and a vasopressor (metaraminol or phenylephrine; ephedrine given the bradycardia) with reassessment","Identifies multiple contributors: sympathetic block, drugs (ACEi, β-blocker, calcium channel blocker), hypovolaemia/bleeding, hypothermia, possible BCIS","Checks Hb (point-of-care), considers blood transfusion and looks for ongoing bleeding","Considers cardiac causes: ECG, troponin if indicated; BCIS sequelae (oxygen requirement)","Warms the patient","Recognises the risk of delirium and AKI in a frail patient with hypotension and oliguria","Decides on an appropriate level of care (does not discharge to the ward until stable; considers enhanced care)","Hands over clearly (SBAR) to the surgical team/ward"],"domains":"<p>Clinical reasoning (integrating physiology and pharmacology), prioritisation, assessment of risk, information sharing.</p>","model":"<p>Acts first (fluids, vasopressor, oxygen, warming), then thinks broadly: a residual sympathetic block (T8, likely higher for sympathetic fibres), antihypertensives taken this morning, blood loss (Hb falling, may be more to come), hypothermia, and possible BCIS. Orders an urgent Hb, gas and ECG, considers transfusion, continues monitoring in recovery or enhanced care until the block regresses and BP is stable, and plans to hold antihypertensives.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Post-spinal hypotension in the elderly is usually multifactorial: treat and investigate.</li><li>Frail hip fracture patients poorly tolerate hypotension (delirium, AKI, myocardial injury).</li><li>Guideline: avoid hypotension and heavy sedation in hip fracture anaesthesia.</li></ul>"},"case-preop-asthma-consent":{"candidate":"<p>You are in the day surgery preassessment clinic. <strong>Ms Jordan</strong>, aged 28, is listed for a diagnostic laparoscopy under general anaesthesia next week. The nurse has flagged that she has asthma.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> take a focused anaesthetic history relevant to her asthma, explain what will happen on the day of her anaesthetic, and discuss the main risks. You do not need to examine her.</p>","scenario":"<p>The examiner plays Ms Jordan (or an actor does). She has asthma since childhood, uses a salbutamol inhaler about three times a week and a beclometasone inhaler \"when I remember\". She was admitted to hospital with asthma two years ago (no ICU). She had a wheezy chest after an anaesthetic for a wisdom tooth extraction at 18. She takes ibuprofen for period pain without problems. She smokes 5 cigarettes a day and vapes. She is anxious about \"being put to sleep\" and asks whether she might wake up during surgery. She had severe sickness after the last anaesthetic.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(Start) Let the candidate lead the history.</li><li>(4 min, if not covered) \"Is there anything else about my asthma you need to know?\"</li><li>(6 min) \"What's going to happen on the day?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"Will I be awake during the operation?\" and \"Will I be sick again?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"Is it safe for me to take ibuprofen afterwards?\"</li></ul>","features":["Establishes asthma control: symptom frequency, reliever use, preventer adherence, triggers","Asks about severity markers: admissions, ICU or ventilation, steroid courses","Explores the previous anaesthetic problem (wheeze) and PONV history","Asks about NSAID tolerance before recommending ibuprofen","Takes smoking history and advises stopping, at least before surgery","Recommends optimising asthma (regular preventer, bring inhalers, see GP/asthma nurse if poorly controlled)","Explains the day: fasting, taking inhalers on the morning, cannula, monitoring, induction, recovery, discharge with an adult","Discusses risks proportionately (sore throat, PONV, bronchospasm, awareness is very rare) and PONV prophylaxis","Checks understanding and invites questions"],"domains":"<p>Information gathering (structured, focused history), explanation and articulation (jargon-free explanation, answering questions honestly), planning (optimisation and a safe plan for the day).</p>","model":"<p>A calm, structured consultation that establishes her asthma is <strong>partially controlled</strong> (reliever use three times a week, erratic preventer), identifies the previous perioperative bronchospasm and PONV, and agrees a plan: regular preventer until surgery, bring inhalers, salbutamol before induction, avoid triggers, consider an SGA rather than intubation if appropriate, PONV prophylaxis, and stop smoking. Explains awareness is rare (about 1 in 19,000 in NAP5) and how it is prevented, and that ibuprofen is reasonable <strong>because she tolerates it</strong> without wheeze.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Assess asthma <strong>control</strong>, not just the diagnosis.</li><li>NSAIDs are safe in many asthmatics who have taken them before without problems; avoid in NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease.</li><li>Consent is a dialogue: material risks are those that matter to this patient (Montgomery).</li></ul>"},"case-remi-tiva":{"candidate":"<p>You are the anaesthetist for a 55-year-old man having a 6-hour elective free-flap reconstruction. You plan total intravenous anaesthesia with propofol and remifentanil by target-controlled infusion.</p>\n<p>Your consultant supervisor has joined you in the anaesthetic room and wants to talk through your plan before induction.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> explain your choice of technique and drugs to your supervisor, and answer their questions.</p>","scenario":"<p>The examiner plays the consultant supervisor. The patient is fit (ASA 2, treated hypertension), 84 kg, 178 cm. Surgery is expected to last 6 hours. The surgeon has asked for a smooth, rapid emergence and good flap perfusion.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"Why TIVA for this case?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Why remifentanil rather than a fentanyl infusion for a 6-hour case?\" Expect context-sensitive half-time. Offer paper: \"Can you sketch how offset changes with infusion duration?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"Which propofol model will you use, and what will you enter?\"</li><li>(6 min) \"How will you make sure the patient is not aware?\" Expect dedicated cannula, visibility, anti-reflux, alarms, processed EEG.</li><li>(8 min) \"What happens to analgesia when you stop the remifentanil?\" Expect transition analgesia.</li></ul>","features":["States a rationale for TIVA relevant to the case (smooth emergence, PONV reduction, haemodynamic control)","Defines context-sensitive half-time correctly","Explains remifentanil's duration-independent offset by esterase metabolism","Contrasts with fentanyl accumulation after long infusions","Names an appropriate propofol model and its inputs","Describes measures to prevent awareness with TIVA","Plans transition analgesia before stopping remifentanil","Communicates clearly and checks the supervisor's agreement"],"domains":"<p>Clinical reasoning (pharmacokinetic basis for the plan); explanation and articulation to a colleague; planning, including anticipating emergence and post-operative analgesia.</p>","model":"<p>A clear, structured plan that links the science to the patient: \"Remifentanil's context-sensitive half-time stays around 3–4 minutes however long I run it, because esterases clear it; fentanyl's would be several hours after 6 hours of infusion.\" Awareness risks and their mitigation are volunteered, and transition analgesia (for example morphine timed before the end, plus regional or local anaesthetic infiltration if appropriate) is planned without prompting.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>CSHT is a property of the drug <strong>and</strong> the infusion duration.</li><li>TIVA safety depends on the drug actually reaching the patient: see the TIVA guideline.</li></ul>"},"case-resus-periarrest-hyperkalaemia":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mr O'Brien, 70</strong>, with chronic kidney disease on haemodialysis, has missed two dialysis sessions and needs emergency surgery for an ischaemic foot. You are shown his <strong>ECG</strong> and blood results.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> interpret the results and describe your immediate management.</p>","scenario":"<p>ECG: HR 48, broad QRS (0.16 s), tall peaked T waves, flattened P waves. Bloods: K<sup>+</sup> 7.6 mmol/L, Na<sup>+</sup> 136, urea 32, creatinine 890, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 16, glucose 7.2. BP 110/60, alert.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"What does the ECG show?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"What will you do now?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"Explain how calcium works.\"</li><li>(6 min) \"Can we give him suxamethonium for the anaesthetic?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"His ECG becomes a sine wave. What now?\"</li></ul>","features":["Identifies hyperkalaemia on ECG (peaked T, wide QRS, loss of P waves, bradycardia)","Recognises a life-threatening situation needing cardiac monitoring and senior help","Gives IV calcium (chloride or gluconate) immediately to stabilise the myocardium","Shifts potassium into cells: IV insulin with glucose (and glucose monitoring), nebulised salbutamol","Plans removal: urgent dialysis (renal team), stop potassium-containing drugs and fluids","Explains the membrane physiology (resting potential, threshold potential, sodium channel inactivation)","Avoids suxamethonium and postpones non-life-saving surgery until potassium is controlled","Manages peri-arrest/arrest per ALS (calcium, continuous CPR, treat reversible cause)"],"domains":"<p>Data interpretation, response to an emergency, prescribing.</p>","model":"<p>A clear, rapid diagnosis and sequence (<strong>protect, shift, remove</strong>), accurate drug doses with glucose monitoring, and a correct explanation that calcium raises the threshold potential, restoring the gap to the partially depolarised resting potential.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>ECG changes do not correlate tightly with the potassium level; treat changes urgently.</li><li>Insulin–glucose is the most reliable shifting treatment.</li><li>Dialysis is the definitive treatment in end-stage renal disease.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-aaa-rupture":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mr Walsh, 78</strong>, has a CT-confirmed ruptured infrarenal AAA and is on his way to theatre. He is conscious, BP 82/50, HR 110. The anatomy is <strong>not suitable for EVAR</strong>. You are the most senior anaesthetist immediately available; your consultant is 15 minutes away.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> lead the team brief (the examiner plays the surgeon and theatre team) and describe your anaesthetic management.</p>","scenario":"<p>Two 16G cannulas in situ; 2 units of O-negative blood given in ED; group-specific blood available; major haemorrhage protocol activated. Hb 82 g/L, lactate 5. Background: IHD, COPD, CKD (creatinine 160). Weight 75 kg.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) Surgeon: \"Let's get him asleep so I can start.\"</li><li>(3 min) \"What blood pressure are you aiming for before the clamp?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"He arrests on induction. What happened and what do you do?\" (if induction is done before prep)</li><li>(6 min) \"I'm about to take the clamp off.\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What are the main risks in the next 24 hours?\"</li></ul>","features":["Leads a focused team brief: roles, plan, critical steps, blood availability, rapid infuser and cell salvage","Maintains permissive hypotension (conscious, systolic about 70–90) and minimal crystalloid before clamping","Places arterial access (under local if possible) without delaying surgery","Insists on prepping and draping before induction, with the surgeon scrubbed and ready to clamp","Plans a cardiostable induction (reduced doses, ketamine or etomidate or titrated propofol/opioid, vasopressor) with RSI","Manages massive transfusion with balanced products, TXA, calcium, warming and viscoelastic testing","Anticipates unclamping hypotension: volume, gradual release, vasopressors, communication","Plans critical care; anticipates AKI, abdominal compartment syndrome, bowel ischaemia, MI and coagulopathy","Escalates to and updates the consultant"],"domains":"<p>Planning, team working (leadership and communication), prioritisation, managing complexity and uncertainty.</p>","model":"<p>The candidate politely but firmly resists induction before the patient is prepped (\"induction may remove his sympathetic drive — I need you ready to clamp\"), leads a crisp brief, and gives an integrated plan of haemostatic resuscitation and haemodynamic management with clear communication at clamp on and off.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Induction can precipitate cardiovascular collapse in ruptured AAA: prep and drape first.</li><li>Permissive hypotension until proximal control.</li><li>Unclamping hypotension is predictable: prepare for it together.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-airway-difficult-planning":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mr Price, 64</strong>, has a supraglottic tumour and needs a <strong>panendoscopy and biopsy</strong>. He has intermittent stridor on exertion, no stridor at rest. You have his nasendoscopy report and CT.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> discuss your airway plan with the ENT surgeon (the examiner), then explain your recommended technique to the patient (role player).</p>","scenario":"<p>Nasendoscopy: exophytic tumour on the epiglottis and aryepiglottic fold, obscuring the right half of the glottis; cords mobile; airway about 50% narrowed. CT: no subglottic extension; trachea normal. Mouth opening 4 cm, Mallampati 3, previous neck radiotherapy (no). BMI 31. Smoker with COPD.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) Surgeon: \"Can't we just put him to sleep and use a small tube?\"</li><li>(4 min) \"What's your backup if that fails?\"</li><li>(6 min) Patient: \"Why do I need to be awake? That sounds horrible.\"</li><li>(8 min) Patient: \"What are the risks?\"</li></ul>","features":["Interprets nasendoscopy and CT: supraglottic obstruction likely to make mask ventilation, SGA and laryngoscopy difficult; normal subglottis and trachea","Recommends awake tracheal intubation (flexible bronchoscope or video laryngoscope) with HFNO and topicalisation, or discusses alternatives (asleep with double set-up, awake tracheostomy) with justification","Agrees plans A–D with the surgeon, including front-of-neck access (surgeon scrubbed, neck marked) and the tube size","Discusses equipment (microlaryngeal tube, jet ventilation options) and the need for shared airway coordination","Explains ATI to the patient in plain language: why, what it feels like, sedation, local anaesthetic, safety, and alternatives","Obtains informed consent with material risks (discomfort, failure, bleeding, need for tracheostomy)","Plans post-procedure care (airway oedema, steroids, monitoring)"],"domains":"<p>Planning, information sharing (surgeon and patient), managing complexity, explanation.</p>","model":"<p>A collaborative, evidence-informed plan that respects the surgeon's needs, with ATI as plan A and front-of-neck access ready; a reassuring and honest explanation to the patient.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Predicted difficulty with <strong>both</strong> intubation and rescue oxygenation is the main indication for ATI.</li><li>Imaging and nasendoscopy help define the level and severity of obstruction.</li><li>Shared decision-making with the surgeon and patient is part of the exam's assessment.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-cardiac-post-cabg":{"candidate":"<p>You are the resident on the cardiothoracic ICU. <strong>Mr Singh, 68</strong>, had CABG × 3 six hours ago and was extubated 1 hour ago. The nurse calls you because his blood pressure is falling.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> assess the data you are given and manage the situation.</p>","scenario":"<p>BP 78/50 mmHg (was 115/60), HR 118 sinus, CVP 18 mmHg (was 9), cardiac index 1.6 L/min/m<sup>2</sup>, ScvO<sub>2</sub> 52%. Chest drains: 350 mL/h for 2 hours, now 20 mL in the last 30 minutes. Urine output falling. Pulsus paradoxus present. Hb 94 g/L. ACT normal, platelets 110, fibrinogen 1.6 g/L. Epicardial pacing wires in situ.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(2 min) \"What is your differential diagnosis?\"</li><li>(4 min) \"What is your immediate management?\"</li><li>(6 min) \"The echo shows a posterior collection compressing the left atrium. What next?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"He loses cardiac output.\" (examiner shows PEA on the monitor)</li><li>(9 min) \"Afterwards, how would you manage the coagulopathy?\"</li></ul>","features":["Interprets low output with rising CVP and falling drain output as likely tamponade; considers other causes (bleeding, vasoplegia, LV/RV failure, arrhythmia, ischaemia from graft failure)","Calls the cardiac surgeon and senior anaesthetist early; prepares for re-sternotomy","Supports the circulation cautiously: fluids, inotropes/vasopressors, maintain heart rate (pacing) and preload; avoids high PEEP and vasodilators","Uses echocardiography (TTE/TOE) recognising localised collections may be missed on TTE","Follows the post-cardiac surgery arrest protocol (CALS): external compressions, pacing, early emergency re-sternotomy within about 5 minutes","Manages coagulopathy using viscoelastic tests/labs: fibrinogen, platelets, protamine if residual heparin, TXA","Communicates clearly with the team"],"domains":"<p>Data interpretation, clinical reasoning, response to an emergency, team working.</p>","model":"<p>A rapid, accurate diagnosis of tamponade, immediate escalation to the surgeon, avoidance of harmful interventions, and knowledge of the specific cardiac surgical arrest protocol including early re-sternotomy.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Falling drain output can mean clotted drains, not stopped bleeding.</li><li>Tamponade after cardiac surgery may be localised and atypical.</li><li>CALS: emergency re-sternotomy is part of resuscitation.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-icu-referral-ards":{"candidate":"<p>You are the ICU resident. <strong>Mr Ahmed, 45 (actual weight 95 kg, height 175 cm; predicted body weight about 70 kg)</strong>, is ventilated for pneumonia on day 2. The nurse calls you because his oxygenation is worsening.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> review the information, talk through your assessment and changes, and discuss next steps with the consultant (the examiner).</p>","scenario":"<p>Ventilator: volume control, VT 650 mL, RR 18, PEEP 8 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, FiO<sub>2</sub> 0.8. Peak 38, plateau 32 cmH<sub>2</sub>O. SpO<sub>2</sub> 88%. ABG: pH 7.24, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 7.8 kPa, PaO<sub>2</sub> 7.6 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 24. CXR: bilateral diffuse infiltrates. Echo: normal LV function. MAP 70 on noradrenaline 0.1 µg/kg/min.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(2 min) \"What is the P/F ratio, and what does it mean?\"</li><li>(4 min) \"What would you change on the ventilator?\"</li><li>(6 min) \"Despite your changes, P/F is now 12 kPa. What next?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"When would you refer for ECMO?\"</li></ul>","features":["Calculates P/F (7.6/0.8 ≈ 9.5 kPa — severe) and applies the Berlin definition","Recognises tidal volume is too high for predicted body weight (≈ 9 mL/kg) and plateau &gt; 30","Reduces VT to 6 mL/kg PBW (≈ 420 mL), increases rate within limits, accepts permissive hypercapnia (pH &gt; 7.20)","Optimises PEEP (higher PEEP table or recruitment assessment) and considers driving pressure","Excludes other causes: tube position, secretions, pneumothorax, fluid overload","Recommends prone positioning ≥ 16 h/day and considers neuromuscular blockade and conservative fluids","Knows ECMO referral criteria (e.g. severe refractory hypoxaemia or hypercapnic acidosis despite optimal care; short ventilation duration; potentially reversible cause) and the UK national service model","Communicates the plan and prognosis appropriately"],"domains":"<p>Data interpretation, clinical decision-making, managing complexity.</p>","model":"<p>Immediate correction of an injurious tidal volume based on predicted body weight, a stepwise ARDS strategy (PEEP, proning, paralysis) and timely ECMO discussion.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Use predicted, not actual, body weight for tidal volumes.</li><li>Prone positioning is one of few ARDS interventions with a mortality benefit.</li><li>Refer early for ECMO; late referral reduces options.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-neuro-sah":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Ms Novak, 49</strong>, has a WFNS grade 2 subarachnoid haemorrhage from a posterior communicating artery aneurysm. She is booked for <strong>endovascular coiling</strong> in the interventional radiology suite.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> discuss your plan with the consultant neuroradiologist (the examiner), then manage the complication that arises.</p>","scenario":"<p>GCS 14, BP 160/95, HR 80. External ventricular drain not in situ. Nimodipine started. Sodium 134.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"How will you anaesthetise her, and what are your targets?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Why is the blood pressure important before the aneurysm is secured?\"</li><li>(5 min) Neuroradiologist: \"Contrast is extravasating — the aneurysm has ruptured!\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What problems do you expect over the next 2 weeks?\"</li></ul>","features":["Plans GA with a smooth induction avoiding hypertensive surges (and hypotension), arterial line before or soon after induction","Sets BP targets (avoid systolic surges before securing; maintain cerebral perfusion)","Considers the remote environment: equipment, help, radiation protection, transfer","Manages rupture: communicates with the radiologist, <strong>heparin reversal with protamine</strong> (if heparin given), controls BP, deepens anaesthesia/reduces ICP, prepares for EVD or craniotomy","Anticipates vasospasm/delayed cerebral ischaemia, hydrocephalus, rebleeding, hyponatraemia, neurogenic pulmonary oedema and cardiac stunning","Uses nimodipine and maintains euvolaemia","Plans neurocritical care"],"domains":"<p>Planning, clinical reasoning, response to an evolving emergency.</p>","model":"<p>Precise haemodynamic control (e.g. an arterial line, remifentanil or alfentanil at induction), a clear rupture drill (reverse heparin, lower BP transiently while maintaining CPP, communicate with the neurosurgeon), and a good understanding of the later complications.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Transmural pressure across the aneurysm rises with hypertension — avoid surges before securing.</li><li>Delayed cerebral ischaemia peaks from days 4–14.</li><li>Remote-site anaesthesia demands careful preparation.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-obstetric-haemorrhage":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mrs Adeyemi, 36</strong>, had a category 2 caesarean section under spinal anaesthesia 20 minutes ago for a failed induction of labour. The uterus is atonic and she is bleeding heavily. Estimated blood loss is <strong>2,200 mL</strong>. She has had oxytocin 3 units IV and an oxytocin infusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> lead the resuscitation, interpreting the results you are given.</p>","scenario":"<p>HR 132, BP 78/40, SpO<sub>2</sub> 96% on oxygen, drowsy. One 16G cannula. Point-of-care Hb 71 g/L. ROTEM: EXTEM clotting time normal, EXTEM A5 low, <strong>FIBTEM A5 6 mm</strong> (low; a threshold of about 12 mm is commonly used). Platelets 110. She has asthma (salbutamol only).</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) Obstetrician: \"The uterus isn't contracting — what can you give?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"What do these ROTEM results tell you?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"She's becoming more drowsy. Do we need to convert to GA?\"</li><li>(7 min) \"Which products and in what order?\"</li><li>(9 min) \"The lab calls: fibrinogen (Clauss) is 1.2 g/L.\"</li></ul>","features":["Declares a major obstetric haemorrhage and activates the protocol; allocates roles; calls for senior help","Secures large-bore access (second cannula or rapid infusion catheter), sends bloods, uses warmed fluids and a rapid infuser","Gives blood (O-negative or group-specific) early; balanced resuscitation","Chooses uterotonics appropriately: further oxytocin, ergometrine (no hypertension), avoids or cautions carboprost in asthma, considers misoprostol","Gives tranexamic acid 1 g IV","Interprets FIBTEM as low functional fibrinogen and gives fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate","Monitors calcium, temperature and acid–base; uses cell salvage","Decides on conversion to GA when consciousness or cardiovascular stability deteriorates, with RSI and head-up positioning","Communicates with obstetrician, midwife, lab, haematologist and the partner; plans critical care"],"domains":"<p>Response to an evolving emergency, team working, data interpretation, prescribing.</p>","model":"<p>Assertive leadership, early fibrinogen replacement guided by FIBTEM (obstetric coagulopathy is fibrinogen-led), sensible uterotonic choice mindful of asthma, and a timely decision to convert to GA before airway reflexes are lost.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Fibrinogen falls early in PPH; below 2 g/L predicts severe bleeding.</li><li>Carboprost can cause bronchospasm; avoid in asthma if possible.</li><li>Communication loops with the lab are part of the treatment.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-paeds-pyloric":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Baby Oliver, 5 weeks (3.8 kg)</strong>, has pyloric stenosis confirmed on ultrasound. The surgeon wants to operate this afternoon. You are shown his capillary blood gas and electrolytes. His mother (a role player, then the examiner) is anxious.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> interpret the results, explain the plan to his mother, then discuss your anaesthetic plan with your consultant.</p>","scenario":"<p>Capillary gas: pH 7.53, PCO<sub>2</sub> 6.4 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 36 mmol/L, BE +12, Na<sup>+</sup> 132, K<sup>+</sup> 2.8, Cl<sup>−</sup> 86, glucose 3.9. Capillary refill 3 s, sunken fontanelle, wet nappies reduced.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"What do the results show?\"</li><li>(3 min) Mother: \"Why can't they operate now? He keeps being sick!\"</li><li>(6 min) Consultant: \"When would you be happy to proceed, and how will you anaesthetise him?\"</li><li>(8 min) \"What will you watch for after surgery?\"</li></ul>","features":["Identifies hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation, dehydration, borderline glucose","Explains why resuscitation comes first (risk of postoperative apnoea, arrhythmia and instability) — surgery is not an emergency","Prescribes fluid resuscitation and maintenance with isotonic saline plus glucose and potassium, with monitoring","States targets before surgery (e.g. Cl<sup>−</sup> &gt; 100, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> &lt; 28, normal K<sup>+</sup>, good urine output)","Plans NG tube aspiration before induction (supine, left and right lateral), RSI or modified RSI, suitable airway and doses, warming","Plans multimodal analgesia (local infiltration, paracetamol), minimising opioids","Monitors for postoperative apnoea and hypoglycaemia","Communicates with empathy and clarity to the mother"],"domains":"<p>Data interpretation, information sharing (with a parent and colleague), planning, empathy.</p>","model":"<p>A clear, kind explanation (\"Oliver's blood salts are out of balance because of the vomiting; fixing them first makes the anaesthetic much safer\"), correct targets, and a safe, specific anaesthetic plan.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Pyloric stenosis is a medical emergency, not a surgical one.</li><li>The alkalosis can cause postoperative apnoea (reduced respiratory drive).</li><li>Empty the stomach before induction.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-pom-frail-laparotomy":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mrs Green, 89</strong>, has a perforated sigmoid diverticulum with generalised peritonitis. She is septic but conscious and has mild dementia; she is judged to <strong>lack capacity</strong> for this decision today. She lives in a care home (Clinical Frailty Scale 7). The NELA-predicted 30-day mortality is 38%. The surgeon has asked you to talk to her daughter, <strong>Sarah</strong> (a role player), who holds a lasting power of attorney for health and welfare.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> discuss the options with Sarah.</p>","scenario":"<p>Sarah expects \"everything to be done\". As the conversation progresses she reveals that her mother often said she \"never wanted to end up on machines\", and she is upset about the thought of her mother dying in pain.</p>","prompts":"<p>(Role player cues)</p>\n<ul><li>\"She'll be fine — she's a fighter.\"</li><li>\"What are her chances?\"</li><li>\"What happens if she doesn't have the operation?\"</li><li>\"What would you do if she was your mother?\"</li></ul>","features":["Introduces self, establishes Sarah's understanding and role (LPA), and explores her mother's values and wishes","Explains the situation honestly with understandable numbers (e.g. \"about 4 in 10 people like your mother would not survive a month, and many survivors do not return to their previous level of function\")","Describes what surgery involves (ICU, possible stoma, prolonged recovery, delirium) and the alternative of comfort-focused care","Makes clear that not operating is a valid option and would include good symptom control","Responds to emotion with empathy and silence; checks understanding","Avoids giving a personal choice while offering a clinical recommendation grounded in the patient's values","Involves the surgeon and senior colleagues; documents the discussion and any treatment escalation plan","Recognises the legal framework (LPA holder decides in best interests; MCA)"],"domains":"<p>Empathy and compassion, explanation, honesty and integrity, clinical decision-making.</p>","model":"<p>A balanced, compassionate conversation in plain language that brings the patient's own wishes to the centre, presents surgery and comfort care as real options, and reaches a shared, documented decision.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Frailty strongly predicts outcome; numbers should be personalised and explained.</li><li>An LPA for health and welfare can make decisions in the patient's best interests when she lacks capacity.</li><li>\"What would you do?\" — reflect the question back to the patient's values rather than your own.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-professional-error":{"candidate":"<p>You are the senior anaesthetic resident on call. A CT2 colleague, <strong>Dr Lewis</strong> (a role player), tells you that during a laparoscopic appendicectomy they gave <strong>metaraminol</strong> instead of <strong>ondansetron</strong> at the end of the case (similar syringes). The patient had a transient rise in BP to 190/100 mmHg and a heart rate of 45, which resolved within 10 minutes. The patient is now awake in recovery and stable.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> talk to Dr Lewis, then explain what happened to the patient (the examiner).</p>","scenario":"<p>Dr Lewis is upset and worried about their career. The syringes were both 10 mL, handwritten labels, drawn up at the start of the list by a different anaesthetist. The patient, <strong>Mrs Fox</strong>, feels well but asks why her heart was \"racing and then slow\" as she woke.</p>","prompts":"<p>(Role player cues)</p>\n<ul><li>Dr Lewis: \"I'm so sorry — will I be in trouble?\"</li><li>Dr Lewis: \"Do we have to tell her? She's fine.\"</li><li>Patient: \"What exactly happened?\"</li><li>Patient: \"Could it happen again? What are you doing about it?\"</li></ul>","features":["Checks patient safety first (observations, ECG, need for troponin/monitoring)","Supports the colleague non-judgementally, acknowledges distress, and signposts support","Explains the duty of candour and why the patient must be told","Ensures an incident report is completed and learning is shared (not blame)","Identifies system factors: pre-drawn unlabelled/handwritten syringes, similar syringes, handovers, fatigue","Tells the patient honestly and clearly what happened, apologises, explains effects and monitoring, and what will change","Documents the conversation and offers written follow-up","Escalates to the consultant and suggests departmental learning (labels, drug trays, M&amp;M)"],"domains":"<p>Honesty and integrity, team working and support, empathy, task management.</p>","model":"<p>A kind, calm response to Dr Lewis (\"thank you for telling me — let's make sure Mrs Fox is safe, then we'll sort this out together\"), a clear and genuine apology to the patient without jargon, and a systems-based approach to learning.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>The duty of candour applies even when no lasting harm results.</li><li>Most drug errors have system causes: labelling, look-alike syringes, pre-drawn drugs.</li><li>Supporting staff after errors (second victims) is part of good leadership.</li></ul>"},"fcpe-regional-rib-fractures":{"candidate":"<p><strong>Mr Hughes, 81</strong>, fell at home and has <strong>left-sided fractures of ribs 4–9</strong> with a small haemothorax (drained). He takes <strong>rivaroxaban</strong> for AF (last dose 10 hours ago). His SpO<sub>2</sub> is 92% on 4 L/min, RR 26, and he cannot cough effectively; pain score 9/10 despite paracetamol and oral morphine.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> discuss analgesia options with the trauma consultant (the examiner) and agree a plan.</p>","scenario":"<p>eGFR 45; platelets 180; INR 1.3; weight 70 kg. He has COPD and mild cognitive impairment.</p>","prompts":"<ul><li>(1 min) \"He's getting tired. What can you offer?\"</li><li>(3 min) \"Why not a thoracic epidural?\"</li><li>(5 min) \"Show me on this image/diagram where the erector spinae block goes.\"</li><li>(7 min) \"How will you dose a catheter, and what will you monitor?\"</li><li>(9 min) \"He becomes confused overnight. What might be going on?\"</li></ul>","features":["Recognises high risk (age, number of fractures, COPD, poor cough) and the need for good analgesia to prevent pneumonia and respiratory failure","Explains why thoracic epidural and paravertebral block are relatively contraindicated with recent rivaroxaban and reduced renal function","Recommends a superficial fascial plane catheter (erector spinae or serratus anterior) with continuous infusion or programmed boluses","Describes the sonoanatomy (transverse process, erector spinae muscle, spread) and safety (LAST, dose limits, monitoring)","Combines with multimodal analgesia (regular paracetamol, careful opioids, avoiding NSAIDs in CKD), physiotherapy, incentive spirometry, HFNO or NIV if needed","Considers a higher level of care and escalation criteria","Considers delirium causes (hypoxia, hypercapnia, pain, opioids, infection, LAST) and management"],"domains":"<p>Clinical reasoning, assessment of risk, planning, information sharing.</p>","model":"<p>A reasoned choice of an ESP or SAP catheter because of anticoagulation, with clear anatomy, dosing within safe limits, monitoring, and integration into a multidisciplinary rib fracture pathway.</p>","learning":"<ul><li>Rib fractures in older adults carry significant mortality; analgesia is a key intervention.</li><li>Match the depth and compressibility of the block to the anticoagulation status.</li><li>Fascial plane blocks are volume-dependent: watch the total local anaesthetic dose.</li></ul>"}};
